        {
          "question": "q-10832",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-12{x}^{-4}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{\u2032}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(1)=-12(1)^{-4}-1=-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-13$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,3),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(3) & =-13(x-1) \\\\ y & =-13x+13+3 \\\\ y & =-13x+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039842/state.json",
          "file_sha256": "7f6512c6f7178f1d7f8465818cf1ceb5616bc56003f0c8e7390f8c5b00405a0b"
        }
